# NestJS Style Rules

## Decorators Are the Language

NestJS is decorator-driven. Use decorators as the primary mechanism for:

- Dependency injection (`@Injectable()`, `@InjectRepository()`)
- Route registration (`@Get()`, `@Post()`, `@Param()`, `@Body()`)
- Validation (`@IsString()`, `@IsEmail()`, `@IsNotEmpty()`)
- Serialization (`@Exclude()`, `@Expose()`)
- Authorization (`@UseGuards()`, `@Roles()`)
- OpenAPI documentation (`@ApiProperty()`, `@ApiResponse()`, `@ApiTags()`)

Don't fight NestJS opinions by re-implementing patterns manually. If you're writing imperative auth checks in controllers, you're missing guards.

## `@Exclude()` on Sensitive Fields

Every entity or DTO that holds sensitive data must use `@Exclude()` from `class-transformer`. Pair with `ClassSerializerInterceptor` registered globally.

```typescript
import { Exclude } from 'class-transformer';

@Entity()
export class User {
  @PrimaryGeneratedColumn('uuid')
  id: string;

  @Column()
  email: string;

  @Column()
  @Exclude()                  // never serialized in HTTP responses
  passwordHash: string;

  @Column({ nullable: true })
  @Exclude()
  refreshToken: string;
}
```

## `@ApiProperty()` for Swagger

Every DTO field gets `@ApiProperty()` with `description` and `example`. This is non-optional in enterprise context — Swagger is the API contract for consumers.

```typescript
export class CreateUserDto {
  @ApiProperty({ description: 'Full display name', example: 'Alice Smith' })
  @IsString()
  @IsNotEmpty()
  name: string;

  @ApiProperty({ description: 'Unique email address', example: 'alice@example.com' })
  @IsEmail()
  email: string;

  @ApiProperty({ description: 'Password (min 8 chars)', example: 'secure1234' })
  @IsString()
  @MinLength(8)
  password: string;
}
```

## Naming Conventions

| Artifact | Convention | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Module | `{Resource}Module` | `UsersModule` |
| Controller | `{Resource}Controller` | `UsersController` |
| Service | `{Resource}Service` | `UsersService` |
| Repository | `{Resource}Repository` | `UsersRepository` |
| Entity | `{Resource}` (singular) | `User` |
| Create DTO | `Create{Resource}Dto` | `CreateUserDto` |
| Update DTO | `Update{Resource}Dto` | `UpdateUserDto` |
| Response DTO | `{Resource}ResponseDto` | `UserResponseDto` |
| Guard | `{Concern}Guard` | `JwtAuthGuard`, `RolesGuard` |
| Interceptor | `{Concern}Interceptor` | `LoggingInterceptor` |
| Filter | `{Concern}Filter` | `HttpExceptionFilter` |

## No `any`

TypeScript `strict` mode is enabled. All providers, return types, and parameters must be typed.

```typescript
// WRONG
async findAll(): Promise<any[]> { ... }
async create(dto: any): Promise<any> { ... }

// CORRECT
async findAll(): Promise<UserResponseDto[]> { ... }
async create(dto: CreateUserDto): Promise<UserResponseDto> { ... }
```

Enable in `tsconfig.json`:
```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "strict": true,
    "noImplicitAny": true,
    "strictNullChecks": true
  }
}
```

## File Organization

Each feature module lives in its own folder. The folder contains exactly:

```
users/
  users.module.ts
  users.controller.ts
  users.controller.spec.ts
  users.service.ts
  users.service.spec.ts
  users.repository.ts
  dto/
    create-user.dto.ts
    update-user.dto.ts
    user-response.dto.ts
  entities/
    user.entity.ts
```

No mixing of concerns in the same file. One class per file (NestJS convention, matches Angular).

## Async/Await

All service methods that touch the database or external I/O must be `async` and return `Promise<T>`. Never use callbacks. Never use `.then()` chains in service or controller code — `await` only.
